.TH QUATERNIONS 3 "2026-10-10" "fortran_quaternions 0.1.0" "Fortran Library Functions"
.SH NAME
quaternions \- quaternions that behave like the Fortran complex type, with 3D rotations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B use quaternions
.B type(quat_sp) | type(quat_dp) | type(quat_qp) :: q
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Module
.B quaternions
provides quaternion types with real32, real64 and real128 components
.IR w ,
.IR x ,
.IR y ,
.I z
(public, scalar first), representing w + xi + yj + zk.
.B quat_qp
exists only if the logical constant
.B quaternions_has_qp
is true.
.PP
Construct with
.BR quat_dp ( w ", " x ", " y ", " z )
(trailing parts default to 0), from an array
.BR quat_dp ( [w,x,y,z] ),
from a string such as
.BR quat_dp ( \(dq1\ +\ 2i\ -\ 3j\ +\ k\(dq ),
or from another kind.
Assignment from an integer of any kind or a real of the same kind sets
.IR w .
.SS Operators
.B + \- * / ** == /=
between quaternions, reals of the same kind and integers of any kind.
Multiplication is the Hamilton product (ij = k).
.I a/b
is
.IR a *inv( b ).
Integer powers use repeated squaring.
.B ==
is exact.
.SS Functions
All elemental except
.BR aimag ,
.BR to_string ,
and the rotation procedures with array arguments.
.TP
.BR abs ", " conjg ", " real ", " aimag ", " dot_product ", " inv ", " normalize
Norm, conjugate, scalar part w, vector part [x,y,z], 4D dot product,
inverse, unit quaternion.
.TP
.BR sqrt ", " exp ", " log ", " log10 ", " sin ", " cos ", " tan ", " asin ", " acos ", " atan
.TQ
.BR sinh ", " cosh ", " tanh ", " asinh ", " acosh ", " atanh
Principal branches as for complex arguments; on the real axis the vector
direction is i.
.TP
.BR is_close ( a ", " b " [, " rel_tol "] [, " abs_tol ])
True if a == b, or if |a\-b| is finite and
<= max(rel_tol*max(|a|,|b|), abs_tol);
defaults rel_tol = sqrt(epsilon), abs_tol = 0.
.TP
.BR to_string ( q " [, " fmt ])
Text "w + xi + yj + zk", each part written with
.I fmt
(default "(g0)").
.TP
.BR "call random_number" ( q )
Each component uniform in [0, 1).
.TP
.BR "call random_rotation" ( q )
Unit quaternion uniformly distributed over rotations.
.SS Rotations
Rotations are active: the vector turns.
.TP
.BR from_axis_angle ( axis ", " angle "), " "call to_axis_angle" ( q ", " axis ", " angle )
Axis need not be unit; angle in radians, returned in [0, pi].
.TP
.BR rotate ( q ", " v )
Returns q v inv(q) for a 3-vector v.
.TP
.BR to_matrix ( q "), " from_matrix ( m )
3x3 rotation matrix R with matmul(R, v) = rotate(q, v), and back (w >= 0).
.TP
.BR from_euler_zyx ( yaw ", " pitch ", " roll "), " "call to_euler_zyx" ( q ", " yaw ", " pitch ", " roll )
Rz(yaw) Ry(pitch) Rx(roll); roll = 0 at gimbal lock.
.TP
.BR slerp ( a ", " b ", " t )
Spherical linear interpolation along the shorter arc.
.SS Input and output
.B print *, q
writes "w + xi + yj + zk".
.B write(u, \(aq(dt\(dqf8.4\(dq)\(aq) q
uses the given edit descriptor for each part.
.B read(u, *) q
reads text up to and including the k part from one record of an external file.
.SH NOTES
Invalid operations return IEEE NaN or Inf; parsing failures return NaN
components or a positive iostat. No procedure stops the program.
.PP
Quadruple precision is compiled if the preprocessor macro QUATERNIONS_QP is 1;
it defaults to 0 for nvfortran and LLVM flang.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
use, intrinsic :: iso_fortran_env, only: dp => real64
use quaternions
type(quat_dp) :: q
q = from_axis_angle([0._dp, 0._dp, 1._dp], acos(-1._dp)/2)
print *, rotate(q, [1._dp, 0._dp, 0._dp])   ! [0, 1, 0]
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The complete reference is llms.txt in the source distribution and
https://willklausler.github.io/fortran_quaternions/
.SH AUTHOR
Will Klausler
